      "audioFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/05-proteins.mp3",
      "timingFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/05-proteins-timing.json",
      "elevenLabs": {
        "requestId": "mIagSdhTUJ90QVgqNpMv",
        "historyItemId": "BLueCkTjBOMn9U2Te70m"
      },
      "productionAudioFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/05-proteins.mp3",
      "productionTimingFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/05-proteins-timing.json"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-vitamins",
      "index": 5,
      "kind": "section",
      "sectionKey": "vitamins",
      "text": "Vitamin E is the best mark at 10% daily value and vitamin C is 5%. Vitamin E helps protect cell membranes from oxidative damage, but the rest of the panel is tiny or absent. For a processed tomato condiment, there is a little vitamin value, just nowhere near enough to carry the score.",
      "textSha256": "5bde6fdb97744630e4cdab7a53bfef6a149f9cd9a1dcfed6957c702a928e11fb",
      "characterCount": 285,
      "byteLength": 247057,
      "mediaDurationSeconds": 15.412,
      "audioFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/06-vitamins.mp3",
      "timingFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/06-vitamins-timing.json",
      "elevenLabs": {
        "requestId": "WUwYA28lVlAhcSzBgIhZ",
        "historyItemId": "ixh5VZqfRZ0NLyTHKxAK"
      },
      "productionAudioFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/06-vitamins.mp3",
      "productionTimingFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/06-vitamins-timing.json"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-minerals",
      "index": 6,
      "kind": "section",
      "sectionKey": "minerals",
      "text": "Potassium is the best mineral at only 6% daily value, while calcium is just 1%. Potassium supports fluid balance and normal muscle function, but this amount is small. For the misc category, useful minerals can create a reason to exist, and ketchup is not getting one here.",
      "textSha256": "7bb5019756947d95f6c288af5c4b7e79e4a7c20fa4ef710102695d69e1861e91",
      "characterCount": 272,
      "byteLength": 233683,
      "mediaDurationSeconds": 14.576,
      "audioFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/07-minerals.mp3",
      "timingFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/07-minerals-timing.json",
      "elevenLabs": {
        "requestId": "CyBgcpXP5p59zC3pNBMk",
        "historyItemId": "E3xQMjo1LWhZZNYJ1N9J"
      },
      "productionAudioFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/07-minerals.mp3",
      "productionTimingFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/07-minerals-timing.json"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-pros",
      "index": 7,
      "kind": "section",
      "sectionKey": "pros",
      "text": "The upsides first: processed tomato brings a lycopene angle. The USDA record contains about 12 milligrams of lycopene per 100 grams, and processing tomato can make that carotenoid easier to absorb. Strong flavour can replace richer sauces. A little sweetness, acidity, and tomato umami can make a meal more appealing without needing a heavy creamy sauce. Cheap, familiar, and easy to use. It is widely available, needs no preparation, and works as a quick condiment for plenty of everyday foods.",
      "textSha256": "8754821354f6c0d084eb7d1558f7699b0f1aa7d35fa8fde2a0e7c706dad36658",
      "characterCount": 495,
      "byteLength": 417167,
      "mediaDurationSeconds": 26.044,
      "audioFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/08-pros.mp3",
      "timingFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/08-pros-timing.json",
      "elevenLabs": {
        "requestId": "TZVUAwS0MXtntSw85t39",
        "historyItemId": "rQvSX8bcFmA9wVjsg63i"
      },
      "productionAudioFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/08-pros.mp3",
      "productionTimingFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/08-pros-timing.json"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-cons",
      "index": 8,
      "kind": "section",
      "sectionKey": "cons",
      "text": "The drawbacks next: salt stacks up quickly. The USDA record contains 907 milligrams of sodium per 100 grams, which is a real tradeoff hidden outside the visible mineral rows. Acidity can bother reflux. Tomato and vinegar make this a rough fit for some people whose reflux is triggered by acidic foods. Squeeze bottles encourage portion creep. A freehand squirt is easy to underestimate, especially when the sauce is spread across chips, burgers, or several parts of a meal.",
      "textSha256": "c37618d5a616845e1ca80d32e5a81d15e71bc478eb443c40fc3ccc94e9348bb5",
      "characterCount": 473,
      "byteLength": 392089,
      "mediaDurationSeconds": 24.477,
      "audioFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/09-cons.mp3",
      "timingFile": "docs/audio/episodes/ketchup/voice-v1-blocks/09-cons-timing.json",
      "elevenLabs": {
        "requestId": "LjsGvzLcnB5VW58zV8CU",
        "historyItemId": "4VzKN0Dx9IZOWoBESjWv"
      },
      "productionAudioFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/09-cons.mp3",
      "productionTimingFile": "production/episodes/ketchup/voice/voice-v1-blocks/09-cons-timing.json"
    },
    {
      "id": "10-closing_summary",
      "index": 9,
      "kind": "closing_summary",
      "sectionKey": null,
      "text": "Best bits are the lycopene angle, strong flavour, convenience, and some value as a lighter swap for creamy sauces. Weak spots are the sugar-heavy carb profile, almost no fibre or protein, weak vitamins and minerals, plus the sodium and reflux tradeoffs. Best for occasional low-calorie flavour rather than nutrition. Essentially, ketchup takes tomatoes, concentrates the flavour, then adds enough baggage that the useful tomato story cannot save the score.",
      "textSha256": "4ab553c029590ffba0f917e3058f40c6de82984298e7da1a1a04c5cdc929e98b",
      "characterCount": 456,
      "byteLength": 348622,
      "mediaDurationSeconds": 21.76,
